Pad alloca on Mac OS 9 (Retro68 GCC frame layout defect).

Retro68's GCC 16.1 for PowerPC (XCOFF, AIX-style frames) puts a
function's locals from the end of the outgoing argument area, but
returns alloca space at a fixed dynamic offset 8 bytes higher. The top
8 bytes of every alloca block therefore share memory with a spill slot.
In JS_CallInternal the frame's var_refs array sits at the top of the
alloca block, so a closure created from a function such as
`() => { const a = globalThis.x; const f = () => a; return f(); }` found
a spilled register instead of NULL in sf->var_refs[0] and read its
captured variable through a stray pointer. Doubles came back as values
like -3.9e-170 and the benchmarks' loop counts collapsed.

A minimal reproduction (powerpc-apple-macos-gcc -O2):

    void f(int n) { volatile int keep = get(); int *p = alloca(n * 4);
                    p[0] = keep; use(p, keep); }

stores `keep` at 56(r31) while p is new_sp + 64, so for n = 4, p[2] is
`keep`. The proper fix belongs in the compiler; until then every alloca
here gets 16 more bytes (alignment stays 16) and the top is left unused.

--- a/quickjs.c
+++ b/quickjs.c
@@ -48,6 +48,15 @@
 #include "libregexp.h"
 #include "dtoa.h"

+#if defined(macintosh)
+/* Retro68's GCC (PowerPC, -O2) can place up to 8 bytes of a function's
+   locals at the top of the space alloca returns, so a store through the
+   alloca pointer overwrites a spilled local and the spill overwrites the
+   buffer. Pad each alloca and leave the top unused. */
+#undef alloca
+#define alloca(n) __builtin_alloca((size_t)(n) + 16)
+#endif
+
 #if defined(EMSCRIPTEN) || defined(_MSC_VER)
 #define DIRECT_DISPATCH  0
 #else
